  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: SBA's 7(a) Loan Program and Credit Elsewhere Requirement**\n\nThe Small Business Administration's (SBA) 7(a) loan program provides financing to small businesses through commercial lenders, with the SBA guaranteeing a portion of the loan. The program aims to serve creditworthy small businesses that cannot obtain credit from conventional lenders at reasonable terms. The report examines the 7(a) loan program's performance from fiscal years 2007 to 2016, focusing on lender compliance with the credit elsewhere requirement.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. The 7(a) loan program accounts for a small portion of total small business lending, with approximately 7% of the total debt financing available to small businesses.\n2. The program has a high rate of lender noncompliance with the credit elsewhere documentation requirement, with 40% of on-site reviews and 29% of targeted reviews identifying compliance issues.\n3. Lenders often fail to document the credit elsewhere justification, and there may be confusion about how to assess family members' available liquid resources.\n4. The SBA relies on lenders with delegated authority to make credit elsewhere determinations, but the agency does not require reviewers to document their assessment of lenders' policies and procedures.\n5. The SBA does not routinely collect or analyze information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria, limiting its ability to understand lender practices and ensure that 7(a) loans reach their target population.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. The SBA should require reviewers to consistently document their assessments of lenders' policies and practices.\n2. The SBA should use on-site and off-site reviews to routinely collect information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria.\n3. The SBA should analyze information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria to identify lenders at greater risk of noncompliance and inform its selection of lenders for further review.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe SBA's 7(a) loan program plays a crucial role in providing financing to small businesses, but the program's performance is hindered by lender noncompliance with the credit elsewhere requirement. To improve the program's effectiveness, the SBA should implement the recommended changes to ensure that 7(a) loans reach their target population and that lenders comply with the credit elsewhere requirement.",
